WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:13.870
<v Speaker 1>Welcome to a deep dive into Ereza Shakazemi's spiritual ethos, where we hear the mandate sent with the new governor of Egypt in its entirety, delivering a warning about his own soul before it names a single duty of state.

2
00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:19.310
<v Speaker 1>A man who cannot govern his own soul cannot justly govern a province.

3
00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:23.750
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, just, I mean, think about the sheer gravity of that opening reality for a second.

4
00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:24.830
<v Speaker 1>It is massive.

5
00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:24.990
<v Speaker 2>Right.

6
00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:27.470
<v Speaker 2>Imagine you are the one receiving this mandate.

7
00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:29.870
<v Speaker 2>You are about to take over a territory that is

8
00:00:30.110 --> 00:00:32.510
<v Speaker 2>frankly, actively fracturing.

9
00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:33.070
<v Speaker 1>Totally.

10
00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:38.830
<v Speaker 2>There is severe unrest, factions are plotting, the borders are tense, and the treasury is just precarious.

11
00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:41.270
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, the stakes literally could not be higher for you.

12
00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:42.030
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

13
00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:46.350
<v Speaker 2>Power, especially in a highly volatile region, is a wager.

14
00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:48.350
<v Speaker 2>It acts as an incredible accelerant.

15
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:48.870
<v Speaker 1>It really does.

16
00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:55.150
<v Speaker 2>It tempts everyone who holds it to completely abandon their internal governance in favor of external control.

17
00:00:55.590 --> 00:01:01.150
<v Speaker 2>When a crisis is unfolding right outside your window, the inward kingdom is almost always the very first thing abandoned.

18
00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:04.590
<v Speaker 2>You want to secure the perimeter, not your own psyche.

19
00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:06.990
<v Speaker 1>That is the natural human reflex, isn't it?

20
00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:07.630
<v Speaker 2>Oh, absolutely.

21
00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:12.430
<v Speaker 1>If someone hands you the keys to a collapsing province tomorrow, what is the first thing you ask for?

22
00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:14.230
<v Speaker 2>The ledgers, probably.

23
00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:18.990
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, you probably ask for the ledgers, the troop movements, and the emergency powers.

24
00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:21.870
<v Speaker 1>The list you demand is purely material.

25
00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:24.510
<v Speaker 1>You want an army to enforce your will.

26
00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:35.390
<v Speaker 1>You want an overflowing public treasury to fund your administration, or at the very least, a heavy seal of office to legitimize your decrees so people listen when you speak.

27
00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:37.070
<v Speaker 2>Which makes sense on paper.

28
00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:38.670
<v Speaker 1>It does, but the imam

29
00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:44.030
<v Speaker 1>Peace be upon him, hands the new governor of Egypt absolutely none of these things initially.

30
00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:44.750
<v Speaker 2>Nothing at all.

31
00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:45.230
<v Speaker 1>No.

32
00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:54.870
<v Speaker 1>Instead, the longest and most comprehensive letter he ever wrote focuses its entire opening breath on the one territory a governor can lose completely unnoticed.

33
00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:55.950
<v Speaker 2>Which is his own soul.

34
00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:57.030
<v Speaker 1>His own soul.

35
00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:00.310
<v Speaker 2>Which is such a terrifying concept if you really sit with it.

36
00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:00.870
<v Speaker 1>Really is.

37
00:02:00.910 --> 00:02:03.150
<v Speaker 2>Because a lost army is obvious to everyone.

38
00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:05.990
<v Speaker 2>A depleted treasury is mathematically obvious.

39
00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:07.310
<v Speaker 1>You can just look at the spreadsheet.

40
00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:08.670
<v Speaker 2>Exactly, but a lost

41
00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:09.150
<v Speaker 2>soul.

42
00:02:09.790 --> 00:02:20.990
<v Speaker 2>A ruler can lose his internal compass and continue issuing decrees, collecting taxes, commanding troops, and sitting on a throne for years before the external rot becomes visible to the world at large.

43
00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:25.870
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, the decay of the state always lags behind the decay of the ruler's character.

44
00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:26.430
<v Speaker 2>Always.

45
00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:32.590
<v Speaker 2>By the time the institutions start crumbling, the internal architecture of the leader has been in ruins for a long time.

46
00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:37.310
<v Speaker 1>And this brings us to the core genius of the text you and I are unpacking today.

47
00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:47.790
<v Speaker 1>It takes the cosmic vision of a god beyond number, a god of infinite majesty, and brings it straight down to the wooden desk of a working administrator.

48
00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:48.910
<v Speaker 2>Just right there on the desk.

49
00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:49.630
<v Speaker 1>Right there.

50
00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:56.150
<v Speaker 1>It flatly refuses to treat theology as something you quietly ponder in a monastery on the weekends and stake

51
00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:58.950
<v Speaker 1>craft as something you do in the capital from Monday to Friday.

52
00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:00.350
<v Speaker 2>Those aren't two separate things here.

53
00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:07.310
<v Speaker 1>No, the mandate treats the running in Egypt and the saving of the governor's soul not as two different jobs, but as the exact same task.

54
00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:08.630
<v Speaker 2>The exact same task.

55
00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:11.870
<v Speaker 1>Every single practical instruction in this document

56
00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:22.030
<v Speaker 1>who to hire, how to tax, when to forgive, how to manage the agricultural runoff is anchored directly back to a fundamental claim about God and the self.

57
00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:24.910
<v Speaker 2>So, I mean, the inner front is the true battlefield.

58
00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:25.390
<v Speaker 1>It is.

59
00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:33.350
<v Speaker 2>And it is a relentlessly difficult front because, as the text suggests, the soul left to itself leans downward.

60
00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:34.590
<v Speaker 1>Gravity just pulls it.

61
00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:38.230
<v Speaker 2>Requires an immense, conscious, agonizing effort to elevate it.

62
00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:40.990
<v Speaker 2>requires what the mandate frames as divine mercy.

63
00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:43.710
<v Speaker 2>If you don't actively fight gravity, you fall.

64
00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:48.750
<v Speaker 1>You are hitting on a very specific view of human nature that this mandate relies upon.

65
00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:49.550
<v Speaker 2>Okay, unpack that.

66
00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:54.150
<v Speaker 1>Well, the imam tells the governor to curb his passions before he even mentions the word Egypt.

67
00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:55.550
<v Speaker 2>Before he even brings up the job.

68
00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:56.270
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

69
00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:04.150
<v Speaker 1>He does this by referencing the profound confession made by Joseph in English, the soul incites to evil unless God bestows mercy.

70
00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:10.270
<v Speaker 1>The inner front is the hardest possible war for any leader to fight because the enemy wears the governor's own face.

71
00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:11.950
<v Speaker 2>Brutal realization.

72
00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:12.750
<v Speaker 1>It is.

73
00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:19.470
<v Speaker 1>Imagine fighting a war where the saboteur is your own desire, your own whim, your own caprice.

74
00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:26.350
<v Speaker 1>If you lose that fight internally, your office simply becomes a weapon against the very people you were commissioned to protect.

75
00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:28.030
<v Speaker 2>I don't buy that yet.

76
00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:29.870
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, let's really stress test this.

77
00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:31.310
<v Speaker 2>I don't buy that yet.

78
00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:34.350
<v Speaker 2>If power is a trust answerable to God,

79
00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:38.670
<v Speaker 2>What actually constrains a ruler who has simply stopped believing that?

80
00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:39.790
<v Speaker 1>Okay, fair question.

81
00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:42.670
<v Speaker 2>Or a ruler who is having a crisis of faith.

82
00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:50.350
<v Speaker 2>If you are running a state and your borders are collapsing, telling a governor to go reflect on his soul sounds like a dereliction of duty.

83
00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:51.550
<v Speaker 1>It sounds passive.

84
00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:51.710
<v Speaker 2>Right.

85
00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:53.150
<v Speaker 2>Isn't this just private piety?

86
00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:58.990
<v Speaker 2>Private piety remains completely silent on how to actually manage taxes, supply chains, and armies in the real world.

87
00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:04.270
<v Speaker 1>It is the most natural temptation in the world to separate personal piety from public administration.

88
00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:04.630
<v Speaker 2>We do it all.

89
00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:13.190
<v Speaker 1>We are deeply conditioned by modern political thought to view governance as a machine that operates independently of the operator's spiritual state.

90
00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:14.750
<v Speaker 2>We just want the machine to work.

91
00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:15.790
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

92
00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:20.270
<v Speaker 1>We think that as long as the laws are good, it doesn't matter if the governor is spiritually bankrupt.

93
00:05:21.070 --> 00:05:23.070
<v Speaker 1>But I want to steel man your challenge.

94
00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:23.870
<v Speaker 2>Please do.

95
00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:32.030
<v Speaker 1>You are right that if this were just a command to just be a good person in your heart, it would be absolutely useless against the machinery of state violence.

96
00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:32.990
<v Speaker 2>It wouldn't do anything.

97
00:05:33.390 --> 00:05:36.590
<v Speaker 1>But the mandate corrects this assumption brilliantly.

98
00:05:36.910 --> 00:05:41.950
<v Speaker 1>It proves that every external order is inextricably anchored back to the soul.

99
00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:43.470
<v Speaker 2>So it's not just abstract.

100
00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:44.750
<v Speaker 1>Not at all.

101
00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:47.230
<v Speaker 1>The text does not leave the constraints abstract.

102
00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:56.510
<v Speaker 1>The imam instantly provides a rigid legal framework that dissolves the predatory instincts of a ruler, regardless of how they might be feeling on any given Tuesday.

103
00:05:56.910 --> 00:05:57.870
<v Speaker 2>Okay, how so?

104
00:05:58.190 --> 00:06:04.510
<v Speaker 2>Walk me through how a philosophical or spiritual classification actually solves the brutal mechanics of power.

105
00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:06.830
<v Speaker 1>By entirely eliminating the category of prey.

106
00:06:07.070 --> 00:06:07.470
<v Speaker 2>Prey.

107
00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:09.950
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, think about how empires usually classify people.

108
00:06:10.030 --> 00:06:13.830
<v Speaker 1>You have citizens, non-citizens, useful classes, expendable classes.

109
00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:15.710
<v Speaker 2>They rank everyone based on utility.

110
00:06:16.030 --> 00:06:16.670
<v Speaker 1>Precisely.

111
00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:21.950
<v Speaker 1>The mandate instructs the governor that the subjects living in Egypt are of only two kinds.

112
00:06:22.030 --> 00:06:25.790
<v Speaker 1>They are either your brother in religion or your equal in creation.

113
00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:27.790
<v Speaker 2>That is incredibly comprehensive.

114
00:06:27.790 --> 00:06:30.110
<v Speaker 1>It covers every single human being.

115
00:06:30.670 --> 00:06:35.630
<v Speaker 1>By establishing this absolute binary, the ruler is left with no third category.

116
00:06:35.630 --> 00:06:36.670
<v Speaker 2>So there is no outgroup.

117
00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:37.230
<v Speaker 1>None.

118
00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:39.550
<v Speaker 1>There is no subhuman class.

119
00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:42.350
<v Speaker 1>There is no acceptable collateral damage.

120
00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:48.870
<v Speaker 1>There is no alien group outside the circle of kinship that can be squeezed or exploited for the state's benefit.

121
00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:51.070
<v Speaker 2>Because they are either family or they are an equal.

122
00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:51.630
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

123
00:06:51.630 --> 00:06:55.870
<v Speaker 1>You cannot treat a brother or an equal as fodder for your political ambitions.

124
00:06:56.430 --> 00:07:04.670
<v Speaker 1>So the private piety is immediately translated into a public structural constraint on how human bodies are legally categorized and treated by the state.

125
00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:07.070
<v Speaker 2>That completely shifts the lens.

126
00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:07.950
<v Speaker 1>It really does.

127
00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:12.710
<v Speaker 2>The inner disposition becomes the absolute prerequisite for seeing the population accurately.

128
00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:13.310
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

129
00:07:13.310 --> 00:07:20.630
<v Speaker 2>If you view a demographic as a problem to be solved or as prey to be consumed, your administration will inevitably become a hunting ground.

130
00:07:20.630 --> 00:07:21.550
<v Speaker 1>It's unavoidable.

131
00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:24.430
<v Speaker 2>But if you view them as kin or equals,

132
00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:27.710
<v Speaker 2>Your administration is forced to become a protective shelter.

133
00:07:27.710 --> 00:07:28.550
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

134
00:07:28.670 --> 00:07:30.430
<v Speaker 2>But here is the practical friction, though.

135
00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:31.310
<v Speaker 1>What's that?

136
00:07:31.630 --> 00:07:33.950
<v Speaker 2>How does a ruler maintain that perspective?

137
00:07:34.190 --> 00:07:35.630
<v Speaker 1>Oh, that's the real test.

138
00:07:35.630 --> 00:07:36.030
<v Speaker 2>Right.

139
00:07:36.190 --> 00:07:47.310
<v Speaker 2>Because the sheer weight of authority, the fact that thousands of people stand up when you enter a room constantly whispers that you are, in fact, superior to those you govern.

140
00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:51.070
<v Speaker 1>This is where we need to look at the first of our three governing analogies.

141
00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:53.150
<v Speaker 1>The ladder of authority.

142
00:07:53.190 --> 00:07:54.190
<v Speaker 2>The ladder of authority.

143
00:07:54.510 --> 00:08:02.750
<v Speaker 1>To combat that constant whisper of superiority, the imam constructs a vivid vertical reality for the governor to hold in his mind.

144
00:08:02.910 --> 00:08:04.030
<v Speaker 2>A vertical reality.

145
00:08:04.030 --> 00:08:05.950
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, picture an endless ladder.

146
00:08:05.950 --> 00:08:06.950
<v Speaker 2>Okay, I'm picturing it.

147
00:08:06.950 --> 00:08:11.150
<v Speaker 1>Every single rung of authority is answerable to the one directly above it.

148
00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:12.670
<v Speaker 2>So there's always someone higher.

149
00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:13.230
<v Speaker 1>Always.

150
00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:16.190
<v Speaker 1>The governor is above the people, yes, absolutely.

151
00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:20.990
<v Speaker 1>But the appointer is above the governor and God is above the appointer.

152
00:08:21.070 --> 00:08:22.030
<v Speaker 2>So it just keeps going up.

153
00:08:22.190 --> 00:08:23.070
<v Speaker 1>It does.

154
00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:33.230
<v Speaker 1>To forget the top of the ladder is to imagine you are standing on the solid ground of your own independent supremacy when in reality you are suspended in midair.

155
00:08:33.230 --> 00:08:34.350
<v Speaker 2>Suspended in midair.

156
00:08:34.350 --> 00:08:38.910
<v Speaker 2>That is a terrifying physical image for anyone holding office.

157
00:08:38.910 --> 00:08:39.790
<v Speaker 1>It's meant to be.

158
00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:44.550
<v Speaker 2>It strips away the illusion of absolute autonomy that power gives you.

159
00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:45.550
<v Speaker 2>aren't the owner of the house.

160
00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:46.430
<v Speaker 1>No, you're not.

161
00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:51.870
<v Speaker 2>Or merely a steward and the property belongs to someone else who is going to return and ask for an audit.

162
00:08:52.590 --> 00:08:54.030
<v Speaker 2>The reckoning is unavoidable.

163
00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:58.190
<v Speaker 1>And that reckoning addresses the specific psychological diagnosis of pride.

164
00:08:58.190 --> 00:09:00.350
<v Speaker 2>Pride is the root issue here, isn't it?

165
00:09:00.350 --> 00:09:02.270
<v Speaker 1>Is the absolute core issue.

166
00:09:02.350 --> 00:09:05.870
<v Speaker 1>In this framework, we have to understand that pride is not just a character flaw.

167
00:09:06.190 --> 00:09:07.310
<v Speaker 2>It's not just arrogance.

168
00:09:07.310 --> 00:09:11.630
<v Speaker 1>No, it's not a minor vice, like being a bit messy or occasionally rude.

169
00:09:11.950 --> 00:09:14.830
<v Speaker 1>Pride is a literal war declared against God.

170
00:09:15.070 --> 00:09:15.550
<v Speaker 2>A war.

171
00:09:16.030 --> 00:09:17.950
<v Speaker 1>It is a rival claim to greatness.

172
00:09:18.430 --> 00:09:24.590
<v Speaker 1>The new governor is explicitly warned not to say to himself, I have been given authority, so I command and am obeyed.

173
00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:27.070
<v Speaker 2>Because that sought alone is dangerous.

174
00:09:27.230 --> 00:09:33.670
<v Speaker 1>That specific sentence, that specific internal monologue is the seed of all political corruption.

175
00:09:33.790 --> 00:09:36.030
<v Speaker 2>It just poisons the well immediately.

176
00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:40.510
<v Speaker 1>It weakens the religion and draws misfortune near because it intoxicates the ruler.

177
00:09:40.750 --> 00:09:42.910
<v Speaker 2>It is the intoxication of agency.

178
00:09:42.950 --> 00:09:43.950
<v Speaker 1>That's a great way to put it.

179
00:09:44.190 --> 00:09:45.870
<v Speaker 2>Think about it from the perspective of

180
00:09:45.990 --> 00:09:47.710
<v Speaker 2>someone who has just taken office.

181
00:09:48.670 --> 00:09:57.470
<v Speaker 2>When people leap to obey your commands, when your signature moves millions of dollars or mobilizes thousands of troops, you begin to hallucinate.

182
00:09:57.470 --> 00:09:58.990
<v Speaker 1>You lose touch with reality.

183
00:09:58.990 --> 00:10:05.230
<v Speaker 2>You begin to believe you are the actual source of power rather than just a temporary conduit for it.

184
00:10:05.230 --> 00:10:05.750
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

185
00:10:05.750 --> 00:10:08.430
<v Speaker 2>But what is the actual prescription for that kind of pride?

186
00:10:08.590 --> 00:10:11.390
<v Speaker 2>Because history shows us that suppression rarely works.

187
00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:13.150
<v Speaker 1>It almost never works.

188
00:10:13.150 --> 00:10:22.350
<v Speaker 2>If you just try to push the ego down through sheer willpower, it usually just mutates and returns in much more insidious, passive-aggressive ways.

189
00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:24.430
<v Speaker 1>You are entirely right that suppression doesn't work.

190
00:10:24.430 --> 00:10:26.510
<v Speaker 1>The cure provided here is not suppression.

191
00:10:26.630 --> 00:10:27.470
<v Speaker 2>What is it then?

192
00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:30.030
<v Speaker 1>It is a radical recalibration of vision.

193
00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:30.830
<v Speaker 2>Recalibration.

194
00:10:30.830 --> 00:10:36.870
<v Speaker 1>Yes, the cure for feeling large is forcing yourself to look at something genuinely infinitely large.

195
00:10:36.870 --> 00:10:38.350
<v Speaker 2>Just looking at the scale of things.

196
00:10:38.350 --> 00:10:38.590
<v Speaker 1>Right.

197
00:10:39.070 --> 00:10:41.470
<v Speaker 1>The mandate tells the governor that if his newfound

198
00:10:41.590 --> 00:10:52.430
<v Speaker 1>authority breeds vanity or a sense of grandeur, he must immediately look at the greatness of God's dominion above him and realize his own profound, almost comical limitations.

199
00:10:52.510 --> 00:10:53.550
<v Speaker 2>Zooming all the way out.

200
00:10:53.710 --> 00:10:58.030
<v Speaker 1>The governor is reminded that he cannot even command a single heartbeat in his own chest.

201
00:10:58.270 --> 00:11:00.110
<v Speaker 2>That is such a striking point.

202
00:11:00.110 --> 00:11:00.750
<v Speaker 1>It is.

203
00:11:01.070 --> 00:11:04.670
<v Speaker 1>His own pulse operates completely outside his jurisdiction.

204
00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:11.510
<v Speaker 1>How can a man whose own internal organs do not take his orders claim absolute sovereignty over a massive nature?

205
00:11:11.510 --> 00:11:13.070
<v Speaker 2>It's absurd when you frame it like that.

206
00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:15.710
<v Speaker 1>It restores what the imam calls good sense.

207
00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:19.270
<v Speaker 1>Pride is diagnosed here as a lapse in basic rationality.

208
00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:20.190
<v Speaker 2>And like a delusion.

209
00:11:20.270 --> 00:11:24.350
<v Speaker 1>Exactly, a break from reality long before it is judged as a moral failure.

210
00:11:24.350 --> 00:11:25.710
<v Speaker 2>It is an invitation to awe.

211
00:11:25.950 --> 00:11:26.430
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

212
00:11:26.510 --> 00:11:29.470
<v Speaker 2>And awe might be the only natural antidote to arrogance.

213
00:11:29.950 --> 00:11:33.150
<v Speaker 2>When you truly perceive the staggering scale of the cosmos,

214
00:11:33.550 --> 00:11:37.790
<v Speaker 2>your own temporary political appointment shrinks back to its proper fragile size.

215
00:11:37.950 --> 00:11:39.950
<v Speaker 1>It really puts things in perspective.

216
00:11:39.950 --> 00:11:41.630
<v Speaker 2>This sets the foundation perfectly.

217
00:11:42.030 --> 00:11:44.750
<v Speaker 2>The governor's soul is theoretically in check.

218
00:11:44.910 --> 00:11:45.950
<v Speaker 1>For the moment, yes.

219
00:11:45.950 --> 00:11:50.750
<v Speaker 2>But let's move from the internal state to the messy external reality of running a province.

220
00:11:51.550 --> 00:11:53.550
<v Speaker 2>A ruler doesn't govern in a vacuum.

221
00:11:53.710 --> 00:11:54.710
<v Speaker 1>No, they don't.

222
00:11:54.710 --> 00:11:58.110
<v Speaker 2>He is immediately surrounded by people clamoring for access.

223
00:11:58.750 --> 00:12:00.590
<v Speaker 2>The company a ruler keeps

224
00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:05.630
<v Speaker 2>dictates the information he receives, which dictates the decisions he makes.

225
00:12:05.630 --> 00:12:07.310
<v Speaker 1>The gatekeepers are everything.

226
00:12:07.310 --> 00:12:11.230
<v Speaker 2>So how does the mandate navigate the treacherous politics of access?

227
00:12:11.550 --> 00:12:16.110
<v Speaker 1>It navigates it by entirely upending the conventional wisdom of statecraft.

228
00:12:16.190 --> 00:12:17.390
<v Speaker 2>Upending it how?

229
00:12:17.630 --> 00:12:24.430
<v Speaker 1>Well, the imam gives a highly counterintuitive instruction regarding whose contentment the ruler should prioritize.

230
00:12:24.510 --> 00:12:24.830
<v Speaker 2>Okay.

231
00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:28.990
<v Speaker 1>He draws a razor sharp line between the common folk and the elite.

232
00:12:29.310 --> 00:12:30.510
<v Speaker 2>Two very distinct groups.

233
00:12:30.510 --> 00:12:31.270
<v Speaker 1>Very distinct.

234
00:12:31.270 --> 00:12:34.830
<v Speaker 1>Now, a ruler's natural survival instinct is to court the elite.

235
00:12:34.870 --> 00:12:35.390
<v Speaker 2>Of course.

236
00:12:35.390 --> 00:12:44.030
<v Speaker 1>The wealthy merchants, the influential families, the powerful military leaders, because they are incredibly useful and they are positioned right next to the levers of the state.

237
00:12:44.190 --> 00:12:45.630
<v Speaker 2>They have the money and the influence.

238
00:12:45.630 --> 00:12:46.350
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

239
00:12:46.590 --> 00:12:53.470
<v Speaker 1>The mandate says to aggressively ignore the demands of the elite and focus completely on the contentment of the common folk.

240
00:12:54.190 --> 00:12:55.710
<v Speaker 2>Why on earth would you do that?

241
00:12:55.950 --> 00:12:57.310
<v Speaker 1>It sounds risky, right?

242
00:12:57.310 --> 00:13:04.910
<v Speaker 2>Historically speaking, alienated elites are exactly the people who fund rebellions, hire mercenaries, and overthrow governors.

243
00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:08.590
<v Speaker 2>If you alienate the powerful, you lose your province.

244
00:13:08.590 --> 00:13:15.150
<v Speaker 1>You would think so, but the mandate paints a precise, devastating psychological portrait of the elite class.

245
00:13:15.430 --> 00:13:16.350
<v Speaker 2>What does it say about them?

246
00:13:16.510 --> 00:13:22.910
<v Speaker 1>It describes them as heavy in easy times, resentful of fair treatment, and painfully slow to be grateful.

247
00:13:23.070 --> 00:13:24.830
<v Speaker 2>So they are basically unmanageable.

248
00:13:24.910 --> 00:13:27.070
<v Speaker 1>They are high maintenance, fair weather friends.

249
00:13:27.470 --> 00:13:34.070
<v Speaker 1>They view every concession you make as their inherent right and every boundary you set as an intolerable insult.

250
00:13:34.070 --> 00:13:35.470
<v Speaker 2>A bottomless pit of demands.

251
00:13:35.470 --> 00:13:36.270
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

252
00:13:36.590 --> 00:13:41.710
<v Speaker 1>The common folk, on the other hand, are the actual structural pillar of religion and defense.

253
00:13:41.790 --> 00:13:42.830
<v Speaker 2>They hold the whole thing up.

254
00:13:42.990 --> 00:13:43.630
<v Speaker 1>They do.

255
00:13:44.030 --> 00:13:56.830
<v Speaker 1>The mandate explicitly states that the discontent of the common folk cancels out the contentment of the elite, while the discontent of the elite is easily absorbed and neutralized within the broad contentment of the common folk.

256
00:13:56.910 --> 00:13:59.070
<v Speaker 2>Because there are just so many more of them.

257
00:13:59.150 --> 00:14:04.830
<v Speaker 1>Yes, the center of gravity for a truly just state must always be the many, never the few.

258
00:14:05.070 --> 00:14:11.110
<v Speaker 2>How is this different from ordinary governance advice just dressed up in religious vocabulary?

259
00:14:11.150 --> 00:14:12.590
<v Speaker 1>I see where you're going with this.

260
00:14:12.590 --> 00:14:13.310
<v Speaker 2>Right, because

261
00:14:13.630 --> 00:14:16.990
<v Speaker 2>Machiavelli would also tell a ruler to keep the masses happy.

262
00:14:16.990 --> 00:14:17.870
<v Speaker 1>Yes, he would.

263
00:14:18.190 --> 00:14:26.510
<v Speaker 2>He argues that a prince must avoid being hated by the people to ensure a stable throne because a fortress cannot save a ruler who is hated by his subjects.

264
00:14:26.510 --> 00:14:26.670
<v Speaker 1>True.

265
00:14:26.670 --> 00:14:28.750
<v Speaker 2>Isn't this just pragmatic politics?

266
00:14:29.150 --> 00:14:31.670
<v Speaker 2>Keep the masses fed so they don't drag you into the street.

267
00:14:31.790 --> 00:14:37.510
<v Speaker 1>It is a vital challenge, and it is dangerously easy to misread this text as pure pragmatism.

268
00:14:37.510 --> 00:14:38.750
<v Speaker 2>It sounds very pragmatic.

269
00:14:38.750 --> 00:14:39.470
<v Speaker 1>It does.

270
00:14:39.470 --> 00:14:46.270
<v Speaker 1>And to steelman your point, yes, keeping the masses satisfied is undeniably the best way to prevent a violent revolt.

271
00:14:46.510 --> 00:14:50.070
<v Speaker 1>But the ultimate measure here, the finish line, is completely different.

272
00:14:50.110 --> 00:14:50.750
<v Speaker 2>Different how?

273
00:14:50.750 --> 00:14:54.110
<v Speaker 1>The goal of this mandate is not a stable throne.

274
00:14:54.430 --> 00:14:54.990
<v Speaker 2>It's not.

275
00:14:55.070 --> 00:14:55.470
<v Speaker 1>No.

276
00:14:55.870 --> 00:14:58.590
<v Speaker 1>A ruler could achieve a stable throne through brutal

277
00:14:58.790 --> 00:15:01.710
<v Speaker 1>surveillance, fear, and state sponsored terror.

278
00:15:02.430 --> 00:15:05.110
<v Speaker 1>The goal here is a clean account before God.

279
00:15:05.110 --> 00:15:06.030
<v Speaker 2>A clean account.

280
00:15:06.110 --> 00:15:10.510
<v Speaker 1>And we know for a fact this is the case because of the doctrine of concealment that immediately follows.

281
00:15:10.550 --> 00:15:11.030
<v Speaker 2>Okay, wait.

282
00:15:11.150 --> 00:15:15.550
<v Speaker 1>Which completely dismantles any possibility of a pragmatic security state.

283
00:15:15.710 --> 00:15:18.110
<v Speaker 2>The doctrine of concealment, I need you to unpack that.

284
00:15:18.830 --> 00:15:21.390
<v Speaker 2>How does concealment dismantle a security state?

285
00:15:21.470 --> 00:15:26.430
<v Speaker 1>Think about what a pragmatic Machiavellian security state actually does.

286
00:15:26.430 --> 00:15:27.470
<v Speaker 2>It gathers intel.

287
00:15:27.550 --> 00:15:29.150
<v Speaker 1>It hunts for hitting faults.

288
00:15:29.390 --> 00:15:38.670
<v Speaker 1>It relies on a vast network of informants, blackmail, wiretaps, and exposing the private sins of its citizens to maintain maximum leverage and control over the populace.

289
00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:40.190
<v Speaker 2>The more you know, the more power you have.

290
00:15:40.270 --> 00:15:40.750
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

291
00:15:40.750 --> 00:15:43.670
<v Speaker 1>The Imam commands the exact radical opposite.

292
00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:44.510
<v Speaker 2>What does he command?

293
00:15:44.750 --> 00:15:48.630
<v Speaker 1>He tells the governor to keep informants the furthest away from him and to view them

294
00:15:49.070 --> 00:15:50.190
<v Speaker 1>members of society.

295
00:15:50.430 --> 00:15:51.550
<v Speaker 1>The most despised.

296
00:15:51.630 --> 00:15:51.950
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

297
00:15:52.590 --> 00:15:58.750
<v Speaker 1>The ruler is ordered to aggressively veil the hidden faults of others so that God will veil his own faults.

298
00:15:58.910 --> 00:16:03.070
<v Speaker 2>But that completely subverts the entire purpose of an intelligence apparatus.

299
00:16:03.070 --> 00:16:04.070
<v Speaker 2>Yes, it does.

300
00:16:04.070 --> 00:16:08.190
<v Speaker 2>An intelligence agency exists solely to uncover what is hidden.

301
00:16:08.190 --> 00:16:09.310
<v Speaker 2>That's its only job.

302
00:16:09.470 --> 00:16:14.990
<v Speaker 2>But here, the ruler is being told that his duty is only to correct what has become public and plain.

303
00:16:15.070 --> 00:16:15.550
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

304
00:16:15.550 --> 00:16:18.630
<v Speaker 2>The hidden faults, the private whispers, the closed door meeting.

305
00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:24.510
<v Speaker 2>the personal failings, belong entirely to God's jurisdiction, not the states.

306
00:16:24.510 --> 00:16:25.310
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

307
00:16:25.710 --> 00:16:31.470
<v Speaker 1>A government that makes it its primary business to expose private failings does not become more just.

308
00:16:31.470 --> 00:16:32.750
<v Speaker 2>No, it becomes a nightmare.

309
00:16:32.830 --> 00:16:35.310
<v Speaker 1>It becomes a paranoid machine of suspicion.

310
00:16:35.870 --> 00:16:43.710
<v Speaker 1>The mandate warns that a slanderer or an informant is fundamentally A deceiver, even if they appear to be giving sincere advice to protect the state.

311
00:16:43.710 --> 00:16:45.950
<v Speaker 2>Because their method is inherently toxic.

312
00:16:46.110 --> 00:16:48.350
<v Speaker 1>The spiritual reciprocity here is absolute.

313
00:16:48.510 --> 00:16:52.750
<v Speaker 1>The concealment you extend to your subjects in this world is the exact measure of concealment.

314
00:16:52.830 --> 00:16:55.710
<v Speaker 1>God will extend to your own glaring faults in the next.

315
00:16:55.710 --> 00:16:57.150
<v Speaker 2>That is a massive tradeoff.

316
00:16:57.390 --> 00:16:58.030
<v Speaker 1>It is.

317
00:16:58.670 --> 00:17:04.910
<v Speaker 1>This proves beyond a shadow of a doubt that the goal is a clean spiritual account, not just political survival.

318
00:17:04.990 --> 00:17:06.870
<v Speaker 2>Right, because if it were just survival.

319
00:17:06.870 --> 00:17:10.030
<v Speaker 1>A pragmatic survivor would keep the informants close.

320
00:17:10.510 --> 00:17:13.470
<v Speaker 1>A spiritually accountable leader banishes them.

321
00:17:13.630 --> 00:17:21.150
<v Speaker 2>It requires an almost unimaginable amount of trust to govern a volatile province without leaning on a network of informants.

322
00:17:21.150 --> 00:17:22.910
<v Speaker 1>It's an incredible leap of faith.

323
00:17:23.070 --> 00:17:25.790
<v Speaker 2>It means accepting A terrifying degree of vulnerability.

324
00:17:26.470 --> 00:17:30.030
<v Speaker 2>You are consciously choosing to be blind to certain things on purpose.

325
00:17:30.030 --> 00:17:30.750
<v Speaker 1>You are.

326
00:17:30.750 --> 00:17:34.710
<v Speaker 2>Which makes who you actually let into your room incredibly important.

327
00:17:34.710 --> 00:17:37.230
<v Speaker 1>And that leads directly to the theology of hiring.

328
00:17:37.470 --> 00:17:38.670
<v Speaker 2>The theology of hiring.

329
00:17:38.670 --> 00:17:39.390
<v Speaker 2>I like that phrase.

330
00:17:39.390 --> 00:17:42.110
<v Speaker 1>The mandate doesn't just give a limp of good resume qualities.

331
00:17:42.430 --> 00:17:47.950
<v Speaker 1>It explicitly forbids the hiring of misers, cowards, and greedy men into your inner circle.

332
00:17:47.950 --> 00:17:49.470
<v Speaker 2>Okay, those seem like bad traits.

333
00:17:49.470 --> 00:17:53.070
<v Speaker 1>But it does not just list them as having annoying personality traits.

334
00:17:53.310 --> 00:17:56.150
<v Speaker 1>It diagnoses all three of them as suffering from the exact

335
00:17:56.270 --> 00:17:58.270
<v Speaker 1>same underlying spiritual disease.

336
00:17:58.270 --> 00:17:59.070
<v Speaker 2>Which is what?

337
00:17:59.390 --> 00:18:02.270
<v Speaker 2>do a miser, a coward, and a greedy man have in common?

338
00:18:02.510 --> 00:18:04.670
<v Speaker 1>A catastrophically bad opinion of God.

339
00:18:04.830 --> 00:18:05.030
<v Speaker 2>Really.

340
00:18:05.030 --> 00:18:05.870
<v Speaker 1>Let's break it down.

341
00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:06.990
<v Speaker 1>Think of the miser.

342
00:18:07.590 --> 00:18:14.910
<v Speaker 1>The miser refuses to spend fearing poverty because he fundamentally believes God's provision is scarce and unreliable.

343
00:18:14.910 --> 00:18:16.430
<v Speaker 2>He doesn't trust that there will be enough.

344
00:18:16.510 --> 00:18:16.990
<v Speaker 1>Right.

345
00:18:17.230 --> 00:18:18.350
<v Speaker 1>Now think of the coward.

346
00:18:18.990 --> 00:18:25.070
<v Speaker 1>The coward fears defeat and flees from duty, believing God's protection is weak or non-existent.

347
00:18:25.070 --> 00:18:26.150
<v Speaker 2>He doesn't trust he'll be saved.

348
00:18:26.430 --> 00:18:27.790
<v Speaker 1>The greedy man.

349
00:18:28.030 --> 00:18:34.910
<v Speaker 1>He hoards wealth obsessively, believing God is not generous enough to sustain him without his own frantic hoarding.

350
00:18:34.990 --> 00:18:37.630
<v Speaker 2>So all three are rooted in a lack of trust.

351
00:18:37.710 --> 00:18:38.510
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

352
00:18:38.750 --> 00:18:44.910
<v Speaker 1>If you surround yourself with these men, their internal spiritual scarcity will rapidly infect your public policy.

353
00:18:44.990 --> 00:18:48.030
<v Speaker 2>The policy just becomes a projection of the minister's internal theology.

354
00:18:48.030 --> 00:18:48.670
<v Speaker 2>Completely.

355
00:18:48.750 --> 00:18:51.870
<v Speaker 2>If your cabinet believes in a cold, stingy universe,

356
00:18:52.990 --> 00:18:56.430
<v Speaker 2>They will inevitably create a cold, stingy administration.

357
00:18:56.430 --> 00:18:58.110
<v Speaker 1>Because that's all they know how to bill.

358
00:18:58.190 --> 00:19:05.230
<v Speaker 2>If you have a miser managing your infrastructure, he will tell you not to fund public works because, well, we might need the money later.

359
00:19:05.270 --> 00:19:06.150
<v Speaker 1>He'll hoard the budget.

360
00:19:06.270 --> 00:19:12.430
<v Speaker 2>If you have a coward advising your foreign policy, he will tell you to compromise the state's integrity

361
00:19:12.950 --> 00:19:15.310
<v Speaker 2>and capitulate to enemies out of sheer panic.

362
00:19:15.470 --> 00:19:17.390
<v Speaker 1>He'll surrender at the first sign of trouble.

363
00:19:17.630 --> 00:19:23.390
<v Speaker 2>And if a greedy man manages your revenue, he will dress up extortion and theft as lawful taxation.

364
00:19:23.470 --> 00:19:24.750
<v Speaker 1>He absolutely will.

365
00:19:24.750 --> 00:19:30.590
<v Speaker 2>Are there any absolute bans on who can be hired regardless of their personality?

366
00:19:30.590 --> 00:19:31.070
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

367
00:19:31.150 --> 00:19:39.430
<v Speaker 1>The harshest, most unbending ban in the entire letter is on ministers who served evil rulers before you arrived.

368
00:19:39.430 --> 00:19:40.190
<v Speaker 2>The holdovers.

369
00:19:40.190 --> 00:19:42.630
<v Speaker 1>The mandate is uncompromising here, says

370
00:19:42.630 --> 00:19:43.630
<v Speaker 1>directly to the governor.

371
00:19:43.870 --> 00:19:47.710
<v Speaker 1>Do not let such men into your inner circle under any circumstances.

372
00:19:47.710 --> 00:19:49.150
<v Speaker 2>Under any circumstances.

373
00:19:49.150 --> 00:19:49.710
<v Speaker 1>None.

374
00:19:50.070 --> 00:19:52.510
<v Speaker 1>They are helpers of sinners and brothers of tyrants.

375
00:19:52.510 --> 00:19:54.030
<v Speaker 2>I have to push back hard on this one.

376
00:19:54.110 --> 00:19:54.550
<v Speaker 1>Go ahead.

377
00:19:54.550 --> 00:19:56.270
<v Speaker 2>What if they are incredibly competent?

378
00:19:56.270 --> 00:19:57.470
<v Speaker 1>It's a common argument.

379
00:19:57.470 --> 00:20:02.270
<v Speaker 2>Running a massive bureaucracy like Egypt's requires deep institutional knowledge.

380
00:20:02.510 --> 00:20:08.670
<v Speaker 2>A former minister knows how the grain silos work, how the tax routes are mapped, where the supply chain bottlenecks are.

381
00:20:08.750 --> 00:20:09.950
<v Speaker 1>They know where the bodies are buried.

382
00:20:10.270 --> 00:20:11.070
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

383
00:20:11.230 --> 00:20:19.990
<v Speaker 2>If you fire the entire middle and upper management just because they worked for the last guy, you are going to plunge the province into administrative chaos.

384
00:20:20.070 --> 00:20:22.190
<v Speaker 1>It seems like a huge unforced error.

385
00:20:22.190 --> 00:20:26.590
<v Speaker 2>Isn't it a massive risk to discard that level of competence?

386
00:20:26.590 --> 00:20:29.190
<v Speaker 1>It seems like managerial suicide, doesn't it?

387
00:20:29.190 --> 00:20:29.550
<v Speaker 2>Does.

388
00:20:29.710 --> 00:20:37.910
<v Speaker 1>But the mandate argues that their technical competence is entirely irrelevant because their fundamental formation is corrupted.

389
00:20:37.910 --> 00:20:38.950
<v Speaker 2>Their formation.

390
00:20:38.950 --> 00:20:42.430
<v Speaker 1>Yes, they have been shaped by the machinery of oppression.

391
00:20:42.670 --> 00:20:44.750
<v Speaker 1>Their muscle memory is injustice.

392
00:20:44.750 --> 00:20:46.350
<v Speaker 2>It's baked into how they operate.

393
00:20:46.510 --> 00:20:49.990
<v Speaker 1>Their default reflex when faced with a problem is to squeeze

394
00:20:50.070 --> 00:20:50.670
<v Speaker 1>is the weak.

395
00:20:50.910 --> 00:20:58.750
<v Speaker 1>The imam insists that you can always find substitutes of equal or greater intellectual ability who carry none of that inherited guilt and none of those toxic reflexes.

396
00:20:58.750 --> 00:21:00.670
<v Speaker 2>So there's always someone else who can do the job.

397
00:21:00.670 --> 00:21:01.230
<v Speaker 1>Always.

398
00:21:01.310 --> 00:21:05.710
<v Speaker 1>And crucially, he argues that those untainted men will cost you far less in the long run.

399
00:21:05.870 --> 00:21:06.670
<v Speaker 2>Cost less how?

400
00:21:06.910 --> 00:21:14.030
<v Speaker 1>Both materially in terms of corruption and spiritually in terms of your own complicity in their inevitable abuses.

401
00:21:14.270 --> 00:21:19.990
<v Speaker 2>Okay, so you have purged your cabinet of the cowards, the misers and the whole

402
00:21:20.190 --> 00:21:22.190
<v Speaker 2>from the previous tyrannical regime.

403
00:21:22.350 --> 00:21:23.470
<v Speaker 1>Your team is clean.

404
00:21:23.550 --> 00:21:24.910
<v Speaker 2>Your inner circle is solid.

405
00:21:25.230 --> 00:21:28.350
<v Speaker 2>But now you have to actually govern the physical dirt of the province.

406
00:21:28.350 --> 00:21:29.630
<v Speaker 1>The actual land.

407
00:21:29.630 --> 00:21:29.990
<v Speaker 2>Right.

408
00:21:30.270 --> 00:21:33.950
<v Speaker 2>Let's shift our focus to the Earth itself and the revenue it generates.

409
00:21:34.510 --> 00:21:38.830
<v Speaker 2>Every state, no matter how spiritually grounded, requires capital to function.

410
00:21:38.910 --> 00:21:41.070
<v Speaker 1>You can't run a state on just good intentions.

411
00:21:41.070 --> 00:21:41.630
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

412
00:21:41.630 --> 00:21:46.030
<v Speaker 2>You have to pay the scribes, you have to pave the roads, you have to maintain the border defenses.

413
00:21:46.030 --> 00:21:53.630
<v Speaker 1>Yes, and the mandate outlines 4 specific, highly practical tasks for the governor regarding the physical province.

414
00:21:53.630 --> 00:21:54.110
<v Speaker 2>What are they?

415
00:21:54.430 --> 00:21:59.150
<v Speaker 1>Gather its revenues, fight its enemies, seek the welfare of its people, and prosper its land.

416
00:21:59.150 --> 00:22:00.390
<v Speaker 2>Okay, those sound standard.

417
00:22:00.390 --> 00:22:00.830
<v Speaker 1>They are.

418
00:22:01.390 --> 00:22:04.150
<v Speaker 1>Now, any standard imperial manual from

419
00:22:04.230 --> 00:22:08.830
<v Speaker 1>from Rome to the modern era would fiercely prioritize the first two taxes and war.

420
00:22:08.910 --> 00:22:10.670
<v Speaker 2>Get the money, fight the wars.

421
00:22:10.990 --> 00:22:13.310
<v Speaker 1>Extract the wealth, kill the threats.

422
00:22:13.790 --> 00:22:23.470
<v Speaker 1>But the imam systematically brackets gathering revenue and fighting enemies and strictly subordinates both of them to the welfare of the people and the prospering of the land.

423
00:22:23.630 --> 00:22:26.990
<v Speaker 2>So he is completely inverting the extractive model of rule.

424
00:22:26.990 --> 00:22:28.190
<v Speaker 1>Completely upside down.

425
00:22:28.510 --> 00:22:33.070
<v Speaker 2>The state does not exist to drain the province like a battery for the benefit of the capital.

426
00:22:33.070 --> 00:22:33.470
<v Speaker 1>No.

427
00:22:34.110 --> 00:22:37.870
<v Speaker 2>The wealth of a country is not the gold sitting in the public treasury.

428
00:22:37.870 --> 00:22:38.590
<v Speaker 1>Precisely.

429
00:22:38.670 --> 00:22:44.030
<v Speaker 1>The wealth of a country is simply the wealth of its people held in a slower, more deliberate form.

430
00:22:44.190 --> 00:22:45.150
<v Speaker 2>A slower form.

431
00:22:45.390 --> 00:22:46.030
<v Speaker 2>I like that.

432
00:22:46.190 --> 00:22:56.990
<v Speaker 1>If you drain the people dry to fill the public treasury, you're behaving like a delusional sailor who bails water from one end of a sinking boat into the other and proudly calls it progress.

433
00:22:57.070 --> 00:22:58.910
<v Speaker 2>You're just moving the disaster around.

434
00:22:58.910 --> 00:23:03.630
<v Speaker 1>This brings us back to the core spine of our discussion, which must be repeated verbatim as

435
00:23:04.190 --> 00:23:06.110
<v Speaker 1>from the spiritual to the material.

436
00:23:06.150 --> 00:23:06.750
<v Speaker 2>Let's hear it.

437
00:23:07.150 --> 00:23:11.630
<v Speaker 1>A man who cannot govern his own soul cannot justly govern a province.

438
00:23:11.790 --> 00:23:18.110
<v Speaker 2>Because if his soul is governed by greed, he will view the land merely as an inert resource to be mined.

439
00:23:18.430 --> 00:23:19.790
<v Speaker 1>A battery to be drained.

440
00:23:19.950 --> 00:23:22.590
<v Speaker 2>Rather than a living, breathing system to be nurtured.

441
00:23:22.750 --> 00:23:27.870
<v Speaker 1>And the mandate puts this philosophy into a stark, undeniable economic formula.

442
00:23:28.230 --> 00:23:29.630
<v Speaker 2>An economic formula?

443
00:23:29.710 --> 00:23:33.710
<v Speaker 1>It commands the governor to let his concern for the cultivation of the land

444
00:23:33.870 --> 00:23:36.670
<v Speaker 1>vastly outweigh his concern for collecting the tax.

445
00:23:36.670 --> 00:23:37.790
<v Speaker 2>Vastly outweigh.

446
00:23:37.790 --> 00:23:38.190
<v Speaker 1>Why?

447
00:23:38.590 --> 00:23:44.270
<v Speaker 1>Because, as the text states plainly, no tax will be gathered where there is no cultivation.

448
00:23:44.270 --> 00:23:46.670
<v Speaker 2>Obviously, you can't tax a barren field.

449
00:23:46.990 --> 00:23:52.910
<v Speaker 1>The state is forced to serve the people furthest from its halls of power, the people actually working the soil.

450
00:23:53.310 --> 00:23:55.870
<v Speaker 1>Cultivation must sit above extraction.

451
00:23:55.870 --> 00:23:58.150
<v Speaker 2>This is our second governing analogy, isn't it?

452
00:23:58.150 --> 00:23:58.710
<v Speaker 1>Yes, it is.

453
00:23:58.750 --> 00:24:00.270
<v Speaker 2>The gardener who commands an army.

454
00:24:00.270 --> 00:24:00.830
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

455
00:24:00.990 --> 00:24:07.230
<v Speaker 1>A predatory ruler prioritizes the tribute tax above all else, alongside rapid military expansion.

456
00:24:07.230 --> 00:24:08.830
<v Speaker 2>Right, just grab the money and the land.

457
00:24:08.830 --> 00:24:16.710
<v Speaker 1>But if you read the specific charges in the Imam's order, you are looking at the job description of a master gardener who just happens to command an army.

458
00:24:16.710 --> 00:24:18.750
<v Speaker 2>Think about how a gardener views dirt.

459
00:24:18.750 --> 00:24:20.190
<v Speaker 1>It's his lifeblood.

460
00:24:20.350 --> 00:24:21.550
<v Speaker 2>He doesn't extract from it.

461
00:24:21.870 --> 00:24:22.750
<v Speaker 2>He aerates it.

462
00:24:22.750 --> 00:24:23.550
<v Speaker 2>He waters it.

463
00:24:23.790 --> 00:24:28.110
<v Speaker 2>He protects it from pests, knowing that the fruit is a byproduct of healthy soil.

464
00:24:28.270 --> 00:24:29.470
<v Speaker 1>It takes patience.

465
00:24:29.950 --> 00:24:34.030
<v Speaker 2>In this framework, taxes and war are not ends in themselves.

466
00:24:34.270 --> 00:24:37.310
<v Speaker 2>They are only necessary instruments for making the land thrive.

467
00:24:37.390 --> 00:24:38.750
<v Speaker 1>Only instruments.

468
00:24:38.830 --> 00:24:45.630
<v Speaker 2>If the people complain of a heavy burden or a sudden blight on their crops or a lack of rain or their irrigation canals being disrupted,

469
00:24:46.030 --> 00:24:49.070
<v Speaker 2>The governor is ordered to immediately lighten what they owe.

470
00:24:49.310 --> 00:24:52.510
<v Speaker 1>And this isn't just framed as an act of pity or charity, is it?

471
00:24:52.510 --> 00:24:53.230
<v Speaker 2>Not at all.

472
00:24:53.350 --> 00:24:56.030
<v Speaker 2>It's framed as a rigorous strategic investment.

473
00:24:56.030 --> 00:24:56.750
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

474
00:24:56.750 --> 00:25:03.310
<v Speaker 1>The mandate frames it as a direct investment that will yield massive returns in the future prosperity of the land.

475
00:25:03.470 --> 00:25:04.750
<v Speaker 2>You're investing in the soil.

476
00:25:04.830 --> 00:25:09.390
<v Speaker 1>A prosperous, thriving land can bear future burdens if an emergency arises.

477
00:25:09.910 --> 00:25:14.350
<v Speaker 1>An impoverished, exhausted land collapses under the lightest touch of taxation.

478
00:25:14.350 --> 00:25:15.150
<v Speaker 2>It shatters.

479
00:25:15.310 --> 00:25:19.950
<v Speaker 1>The devastation of the land always, without exception, begins in the souls of the governors.

480
00:25:20.110 --> 00:25:21.550
<v Speaker 2>I want to trace that causality.

481
00:25:21.670 --> 00:25:23.150
<v Speaker 1>It's A fascinating link.

482
00:25:23.390 --> 00:25:27.470
<v Speaker 2>How does the text connect the invisible soul of a governor in a palace

483
00:25:27.950 --> 00:25:30.430
<v Speaker 2>to ruin crops in a field hundreds of miles away.

484
00:25:30.510 --> 00:25:32.910
<v Speaker 1>It draws a direct, unbroken line.

485
00:25:33.150 --> 00:25:38.430
<v Speaker 1>It states that the devastation of the land comes exclusively from the destitution of its inhabitants.

486
00:25:38.990 --> 00:25:46.270
<v Speaker 1>And the destitution of the inhabitants comes about only when the frantic desire to amass wealth rules the souls of the governors.

487
00:25:46.830 --> 00:25:48.990
<v Speaker 1>It is a fatal chain reaction.

488
00:25:49.390 --> 00:25:55.950
<v Speaker 1>Greed is defined here not just as wanting more, but as severe spiritual short-sightedness.

489
00:25:55.950 --> 00:25:57.390
<v Speaker 1>A ruler aggressively

490
00:25:57.590 --> 00:26:04.390
<v Speaker 1>squeezes the land tax out of a struggling populace only when he severely doubts what endures and forgets that he will inevitably be judged.

491
00:26:04.430 --> 00:26:05.310
<v Speaker 2>He's panic courting.

492
00:26:05.310 --> 00:26:12.910
<v Speaker 1>If he truly deeply remembered his mortality, he would never trade eternal spiritual ruin for a temporary spike in revenue.

493
00:26:13.070 --> 00:26:15.470
<v Speaker 2>That brings us to the actual mechanics of society.

494
00:26:15.950 --> 00:26:19.910
<v Speaker 2>The mandate doesn't just offer broad economic philosophy or poetic metaphors.

495
00:26:19.910 --> 00:26:21.470
<v Speaker 1>No, it gets very granular.

496
00:26:21.550 --> 00:26:23.870
<v Speaker 2>It details the specific pillars of the state.

497
00:26:24.350 --> 00:26:27.310
<v Speaker 2>How do you actually organize a society under this vision?

498
00:26:27.710 --> 00:26:31.550
<v Speaker 1>The specific classes that make up the great wheel of society.

499
00:26:31.670 --> 00:26:32.110
<v Speaker 1>Who are they?

500
00:26:32.350 --> 00:26:37.430
<v Speaker 1>You have the soldiers, the judges, the scribes, the merchants, and the destitute.

501
00:26:37.550 --> 00:26:38.670
<v Speaker 1>Okay, five classes.

502
00:26:38.750 --> 00:26:42.590
<v Speaker 1>And it makes a profound theological claim about all these classes.

503
00:26:42.990 --> 00:26:47.030
<v Speaker 1>God has assigned each its due as a binding covenant.

504
00:26:47.030 --> 00:26:48.350
<v Speaker 1>Binding covenant.

505
00:26:48.350 --> 00:26:53.310
<v Speaker 1>The distribution of rights across these groups is not a clever policy the governor invents.

506
00:26:53.470 --> 00:26:55.950
<v Speaker 1>It is a sacred trust he is bound to protect.

507
00:26:55.950 --> 00:26:57.070
<v Speaker 1>Okay, I have to pose a.

508
00:26:57.830 --> 00:26:58.830
<v Speaker 2>Rigorous challenge here.

509
00:26:58.830 --> 00:26:59.310
<v Speaker 1>Go for it.

510
00:26:59.310 --> 00:27:02.470
<v Speaker 2>Can a state actually function this way in the real world?

511
00:27:02.470 --> 00:27:03.870
<v Speaker 1>That is the big question.

512
00:27:03.870 --> 00:27:08.510
<v Speaker 2>He wants soldiers to be treated with the affection of parents for their children.

513
00:27:09.190 --> 00:27:13.550
<v Speaker 2>He wants judges to be paid an absolute fortune so they are completely independent.

514
00:27:14.030 --> 00:27:19.030
<v Speaker 2>He wants the open market to be generally trusted, but hoarders to be violently punished.

515
00:27:19.030 --> 00:27:19.790
<v Speaker 1>Yes, he does.

516
00:27:19.790 --> 00:27:27.310
<v Speaker 2>And on top of all that, he wants the ruler to purposefully surround himself with people who criticize him to his face.

517
00:27:27.510 --> 00:27:27.910
<v Speaker 1>All true.

518
00:27:28.030 --> 00:27:29.750
<v Speaker 2>It sounds like an administrative fancy.

519
00:27:29.870 --> 00:27:30.910
<v Speaker 1>That sounds impossible.

520
00:27:30.990 --> 00:27:35.150
<v Speaker 2>It sounds like a utopian blueprint that would collapse the moment a real crisis hit.

521
00:27:35.310 --> 00:27:38.790
<v Speaker 1>It is a massive challenge to the purely pragmatic, cynical

522
00:27:38.910 --> 00:27:39.470
<v Speaker 1>mind.

523
00:27:40.030 --> 00:27:46.910
<v Speaker 1>But let's look at the brilliant underlying mechanism that is applied uniformly across every single pillar you just mentioned.

524
00:27:46.910 --> 00:27:47.790
<v Speaker 2>What's the mechanism?

525
00:27:47.790 --> 00:27:52.110
<v Speaker 1>It is a two-part mechanism, generous provision and close watching.

526
00:27:52.110 --> 00:27:53.070
<v Speaker 2>Provision and watching.

527
00:27:53.070 --> 00:27:54.750
<v Speaker 1>Let's take the judges and the scribes.

528
00:27:54.750 --> 00:28:03.390
<v Speaker 1>You pay the judge and the scribes so much, you flood them with so much provision, that basic human need cannot possibly tempt them to take a bribe.

529
00:28:03.470 --> 00:28:05.710
<v Speaker 2>So you remove the economic incentive to cheat.

530
00:28:05.790 --> 00:28:08.710
<v Speaker 1>And simultaneously you watch them so closely that the

531
00:28:08.790 --> 00:28:11.470
<v Speaker 1>opportunity for corruption cannot tempt them either.

532
00:28:11.470 --> 00:28:12.430
<v Speaker 2>I see it now.

533
00:28:12.670 --> 00:28:14.030
<v Speaker 2>I have to concede this point.

534
00:28:14.150 --> 00:28:15.590
<v Speaker 1>It's pretty elegant, isn't it?

535
00:28:15.590 --> 00:28:16.590
<v Speaker 2>It's not naive at all.

536
00:28:16.910 --> 00:28:28.990
<v Speaker 2>It refuses the utopian fantasy that good character alone will survive starvation and temptation, and it equally refuses the cynics fantasy that surveillance and fear alone create an honest society.

537
00:28:28.990 --> 00:28:29.630
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

538
00:28:29.630 --> 00:28:32.030
<v Speaker 2>It's a perfectly balanced psychological system.

539
00:28:32.350 --> 00:28:37.070
<v Speaker 2>You saturate their needs so they have no excuse, and you watch their actions so they have no cover.

540
00:28:37.390 --> 00:28:38.190
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

541
00:28:38.910 --> 00:28:42.110
<v Speaker 1>the environment to make it easier to be honest than to be corrupt.

542
00:28:42.110 --> 00:28:43.630
<v Speaker 2>It's behavioral design.

543
00:28:43.790 --> 00:28:45.950
<v Speaker 1>Think about the vulnerability of a judge.

544
00:28:46.350 --> 00:28:49.870
<v Speaker 1>If a judge is poor, he can be bought by the wealthy merchants.

545
00:28:49.870 --> 00:28:50.350
<v Speaker 2>Easily.

546
00:28:50.510 --> 00:28:56.590
<v Speaker 1>If a judge is exposed and unprotected, he can be frightened and intimidated by the powerful military elite.

547
00:28:56.590 --> 00:28:58.030
<v Speaker 2>They'll just threaten his family.

548
00:28:58.270 --> 00:29:08.710
<v Speaker 1>The mandate solves both of these vulnerabilities by commanding the governor to pay the judge generously and to grant him a rank of nearness to the governor himself so that the judge is

549
00:29:08.830 --> 00:29:12.110
<v Speaker 1>safe, shielded, and untouchable by anyone else in the state.

550
00:29:12.270 --> 00:29:13.150
<v Speaker 2>He's bulletproof.

551
00:29:13.390 --> 00:29:19.310
<v Speaker 1>But simultaneously, the governor must quietly and relentlessly watch their affairs through trusted observers.

552
00:29:19.630 --> 00:29:20.950
<v Speaker 2>So there's the accountability.

553
00:29:20.990 --> 00:29:32.430
<v Speaker 1>And if an officer betrays that sacred trust, the punishment is swift, severe, and highly public because showing mercy to the citizens demands absolute severity toward the officer who preys on them.

554
00:29:32.670 --> 00:29:37.870
<v Speaker 2>The mechanism is highly practical, but it requires a governor who is constantly, deeply attentive.

555
00:29:37.870 --> 00:29:38.710
<v Speaker 1>You can't just set it

556
00:29:38.790 --> 00:29:39.550
<v Speaker 1>and forget it.

557
00:29:39.790 --> 00:29:41.550
<v Speaker 2>The system doesn't run on autopilot.

558
00:29:42.430 --> 00:29:45.630
<v Speaker 2>Which brings us to our third analogy, the hub and the axle.

559
00:29:45.790 --> 00:29:49.510
<v Speaker 1>Yes, visualize society as a massive wooden wheel.

560
00:29:49.510 --> 00:29:50.270
<v Speaker 2>Like a wagon wheel.

561
00:29:50.270 --> 00:29:57.710
<v Speaker 1>The various classes we just discussed, the soldiers, the scribes, the merchants, the judges, are the spokes radiating outward.

562
00:29:57.710 --> 00:29:58.110
<v Speaker 2>Okay.

563
00:29:58.350 --> 00:30:00.590
<v Speaker 1>The governor himself is the central hub.

564
00:30:00.910 --> 00:30:02.750
<v Speaker 1>But think about the engineering of a wheel.

565
00:30:03.350 --> 00:30:04.750
<v Speaker 1>The hub turns nothing.

566
00:30:04.990 --> 00:30:08.910
<v Speaker 1>It moves nowhere unless it stays firmly joined to the unseen axle.

567
00:30:08.990 --> 00:30:09.790
<v Speaker 2>It just sits there.

568
00:30:09.950 --> 00:30:13.310
<v Speaker 1>In this analogy, the axle is the real God himself.

569
00:30:13.950 --> 00:30:16.990
<v Speaker 1>If the hub disconnects from the axle, what happens?

570
00:30:17.070 --> 00:30:22.910
<v Speaker 1>The friction builds uncontrollably, the wheel shatters, the cart flips, and the entire society crashes.

571
00:30:22.910 --> 00:30:24.190
<v Speaker 2>The whole thing just breaks down.

572
00:30:24.190 --> 00:30:30.190
<v Speaker 1>The administrative machine, no matter how well funded, only functions if the ruler remains spiritually grounded to the center.

573
00:30:30.270 --> 00:30:32.790
<v Speaker 2>And part of maintaining that grounding, preventing that

574
00:30:32.910 --> 00:30:37.230
<v Speaker 2>destructive friction involves who the ruler deliberately allows to speak.

575
00:30:37.310 --> 00:30:38.430
<v Speaker 1>Communication is key.

576
00:30:38.590 --> 00:30:42.670
<v Speaker 2>We talked earlier about keeping informants far away, but who is brought close?

577
00:30:43.030 --> 00:30:44.590
<v Speaker 2>Who gets the seat next to the governor?

578
00:30:44.750 --> 00:30:50.110
<v Speaker 1>The absolute highest rank in the ruler's inner circle is given to the bitter truth teller.

579
00:30:50.190 --> 00:30:51.070
<v Speaker 2>The bitter truth teller.

580
00:30:51.150 --> 00:31:00.910
<v Speaker 1>The imam commands the governor to elevate the man who most readily speaks the bitter truth and who gives the absolute least encouragement in whatever the governor does that God dislikes.

581
00:31:00.990 --> 00:31:10.670
<v Speaker 2>So the most valuable person in the room, the highest paid, most respected advisor, is the one who consistently makes the ruler the most uncomfortable.

582
00:31:10.750 --> 00:31:11.350
<v Speaker 1>Precise.

583
00:31:11.350 --> 00:31:12.550
<v Speaker 2>That goes against every

584
00:31:12.590 --> 00:31:13.630
<v Speaker 2>ego impulse.

585
00:31:13.710 --> 00:31:19.550
<v Speaker 1>Because a ruler surrounded exclusively by applause rapidly loses the ability to hear reality.

586
00:31:19.550 --> 00:31:21.230
<v Speaker 2>They get high on their own supply.

587
00:31:21.310 --> 00:31:24.110
<v Speaker 1>Excessive praise breeds deep conceit.

588
00:31:24.270 --> 00:31:28.910
<v Speaker 1>The truth teller is the literal anchor that prevents the hub from spinning off the axle.

589
00:31:29.070 --> 00:31:34.830
<v Speaker 2>The text also emphasizes A radical form of accessibility, particularly for the destitute.

590
00:31:35.310 --> 00:31:36.590
<v Speaker 2>How does this play out physically?

591
00:31:36.750 --> 00:31:40.750
<v Speaker 1>This is perhaps one of the most moving and demanding parts of the entire mandate.

592
00:31:40.950 --> 00:31:41.310
<v Speaker 2>How so?

593
00:31:41.550 --> 00:31:42.430
<v Speaker 1>The lowest class,

594
00:31:42.670 --> 00:31:49.230
<v Speaker 1>no resources, the disabled, the afflicted, the orphans, they are the sharpest, truest test of a society's justice.

595
00:31:49.470 --> 00:31:50.670
<v Speaker 2>The canary in the coal mine.

596
00:31:50.830 --> 00:31:51.310
<v Speaker 2>Why?

597
00:31:51.550 --> 00:31:56.030
<v Speaker 1>Because a ruler gains absolutely zero political capital by helping them.

598
00:31:56.270 --> 00:31:57.550
<v Speaker 2>They don't have lobbyists.

599
00:31:57.550 --> 00:32:02.910
<v Speaker 1>They cannot fund his campaigns, they cannot fight in his armies, and they cannot write poems praising his name.

600
00:32:02.950 --> 00:32:05.910
<v Speaker 2>There's no ROI on them in a purely political sense.

601
00:32:05.910 --> 00:32:06.270
<v Speaker 2>None.

602
00:32:06.430 --> 00:32:12.470
<v Speaker 1>Yet the Imam commands the governor to assign them a fixed, non-negotiable share

603
00:32:13.230 --> 00:32:21.710
<v Speaker 1>from the public treasury, explicitly noting that those furthest away from the capital have the exact same right as those living next door to the palace.

604
00:32:21.950 --> 00:32:24.910
<v Speaker 2>Distance from power should not equal distance from provision.

605
00:32:24.910 --> 00:32:25.710
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

606
00:32:25.710 --> 00:32:28.030
<v Speaker 2>But how does the governor actually interact with them?

607
00:32:28.190 --> 00:32:36.150
<v Speaker 1>To ensure this isn't just a bureaucratic exercise, the ruler must physically sit with the destitute in an assembly entirely stripped of guards.

608
00:32:36.150 --> 00:32:37.230
<v Speaker 2>Stripped of guards.

609
00:32:37.470 --> 00:32:38.270
<v Speaker 2>Pause and picture that.

610
00:32:38.270 --> 00:32:39.310
<v Speaker 1>It's hard to imagine today.

611
00:32:39.310 --> 00:32:43.070
<v Speaker 2>For a provincial governor in a time of severe unrest, surrounded by

612
00:32:43.190 --> 00:32:51.670
<v Speaker 2>political enemies, to sit in a room full of desperate people without his armed security detail, that is intense physical vulnerability.

613
00:32:51.670 --> 00:32:52.590
<v Speaker 1>It's terrifying.

614
00:32:52.590 --> 00:32:56.350
<v Speaker 2>It goes against every single instinct of self-preservation a leader has.

615
00:32:56.510 --> 00:33:00.670
<v Speaker 1>The mandate demands it so that the weak may speak without inhibition.

616
00:33:01.310 --> 00:33:06.190
<v Speaker 1>The apparatus of protection is always simultaneously an apparatus of insulation.

617
00:33:06.190 --> 00:33:07.070
<v Speaker 2>It keeps people out.

618
00:33:07.230 --> 00:33:09.710
<v Speaker 1>Think about the physical space of power.

619
00:33:10.190 --> 00:33:24.830
<v Speaker 1>If A destitute, trembling citizen has to pass through massive iron gates, walk past rows of heavily armed soldiers, and stand before a raised throne just to voice a grievance about their stolen land, they will remain silent.

620
00:33:24.950 --> 00:33:25.870
<v Speaker 2>Of course they will.

621
00:33:25.950 --> 00:33:29.070
<v Speaker 1>The physical intimidation will choke the words in their throat.

622
00:33:29.070 --> 00:33:30.830
<v Speaker 2>It's designed to do exactly that.

623
00:33:30.910 --> 00:33:34.750
<v Speaker 1>The ruler must personally tear down the physical walls of intimidation.

624
00:33:34.990 --> 00:33:42.910
<v Speaker 1>The mandate states clearly, a nation where the rights of the weak cannot be rested without fear from the strong will never, ever be blessed.

625
00:33:42.910 --> 00:33:47.150
<v Speaker 2>It forces the ruler to experience the visceral reality of his people directly.

626
00:33:47.150 --> 00:33:48.230
<v Speaker 1>You can't hide from it.

627
00:33:48.270 --> 00:33:54.190
<v Speaker 2>If you wall yourself off behind security details and sycophants, you lose awareness of what your province actually feels like.

628
00:33:54.190 --> 00:33:55.710
<v Speaker 1>It just becomes numbers on a page.

629
00:33:55.790 --> 00:34:00.750
<v Speaker 2>The beautiful begins to appear ugly, the small appears great, and you end up governing

630
00:34:00.910 --> 00:34:05.950
<v Speaker 2>hallucination, a spreadsheet, rather than a living, breathing province.

631
00:34:06.030 --> 00:34:11.870
<v Speaker 1>This philosophy of radical accessibility even extends to how the ruler leads the community in worship.

632
00:34:12.590 --> 00:34:13.390
<v Speaker 2>Even in worship.

633
00:34:13.550 --> 00:34:17.550
<v Speaker 1>The Imam shares a stunning instruction he learned directly from the Prophet.

634
